#!/usr/bin/env briar
# Kalendarium

A window that shows one day of the Roman liturgical year: its date in
Latin, the season and the week, the celebrations that fall on it and
their rank, the colour the priest wears, the cycles of readings and
psalms, and the mysteries of the Rosary prayed on that weekday. Three
buttons walk to the day before, back to today, and to the day after; a
field takes any date; and a panel lists the feasts that move with
Easter in the year on screen, each of them a door to its own day.

The program is two halves that never share memory. The page — HTML, a
stylesheet, a script — knows how to draw and how to ask. The C knows
the calendar. Between them runs one bridge, the *internuntius*, and
everything that crosses it is JSON: the page sends a question, and C
sends back either an answer or a fault. The page does no date
arithmetic at all. That is a decision, and its reason is at the end of
this file.

This is the web-view counterpart of `salutatio2.thistle`, which draws
with the CPU rasterizer and opens its window itself. Here briar writes
the main function, the window is a WebKit view, and the order below
follows a single request: from the window, to the page that sends it,
to the C that answers it, to the probatio that checks the answer
without any window at all.

## Verba: the standing vocabulary

These appear in every rhubarb program and are not glossed again below.

| word | sense | note |
|---|---|---|
| *piscina* | fish-pond, basin | a Roman reservoir. Here: an arena. Memory is drawn from it and it is emptied whole. |
| *chorda* | a string, a cord | a length and a pointer; never terminated by a zero byte |
| *vacuum*, *nihil*, *zephyrum* | empty, nothing, zero | `void`, `NULL`, `0` |
| *si*, *alioquin*, *per*, *redde* | if, otherwise, through, give back | `if`, `else`, `for`, `return` |
| *hic manens* | staying here | `static` on an object at file scope: it stays put for the life of the process |
| *interior* | inner | `static` on a function: not for export |
| *constans* | standing firm | `const` |
| *fructus* | fruit, yield | what a call gives back — *benedictus fructus ventris tui* |
| *culpa* | fault | *mea culpa*, from the Confiteor. Here: the text of an error |
| *argumenta* | things brought forward | what the page sends with a call |
| *probatio* | a testing, a proof | the part of the file that checks the rest |

Numeric constants are Roman numerals: `I`, `II`, `III`, `IV`, `V`,
`VI`, `VII`, `XI`, `LXIV`, `M`. So `LXIV * M` is sixty-four thousand
bytes and `VII` is the length of a week.

## Kalendarium: the words of the calendar

The *Kalendae* were the first day of every Roman month, from *calare*,
to call out: on that morning a minor pontiff announced how many days
remained until the Nones. Debts fell due on the Kalends, so a
moneylender's book of accounts was his *kalendarium*, and the word
moved from the ledger to the list of days itself.

*Fasti* are the days that are *fas*, when it is lawful to do business
before a magistrate; the published table of them became the Roman
calendar, and `fasti.h` is the house library of dates. *Pridie* is the
day before and *postridie* the day after, the words Romans used to
count toward a named day. *Hodie* is today — *hodie Christus natus
est*, the Christmas antiphon.

*Tempus per annum*, "time through the year", is Ordinary Time. A
*hebdomada* is a week, from the Greek for a set of seven. The
*psalterium* is the four-week cycle of psalms in the Liturgy of the
Hours; the *cyclus dominicalis* is the Sunday lectionary year (A for
Matthew, B for Mark, C for Luke), and the *cyclus ferialis* the
weekday year (I in odd years, II in even).

## Clausura: where the words come from

This file contains no `#include`. briar derives the closure from the
symbols used. The table below is the reader's copy, taken from the
output of `briar -partes`; if the two ever disagree, the table is
wrong.

| header | what this file takes from it |
|---|---|
| `json.h` | `JsonValor` and every `json_*` call — the one include written by hand, below |
| `calendarium_liturgicum.h` | `CalendariumLiturgicum`, `InformatioDiei`, `Celebratio`, `AnchoresMobiles`, `ColorLiturgicus`, and the `calendarium_*` questions |
| `fasti.h` | `Dies`, `fasti_parsare_iso`, `fasti_formare_diem` with its `FASTI_FORMA_*`, `fasti_addere_dies`, `fasti_dies_hebdomadis`, `fasti_dies_hodie` |
| `credo.h` | the test harness, in the probatio only |

`piscina.h` and `chorda.h` arrive through those headers, and the
generated main adds `atrium.h`, `internuntius.h` and the bundle of
assets. The whole closure `-partes` lists runs to eighty files: a web
view in a native window brings WebKit's glue, the HTTP control port
that lets `bin/manus` drive the app, and everything those need.

## Fenestra: the window, and the main function nobody writes

*Fenestra* is a window. The tag below is the whole of it:

<fenestra titulus="kalendarium" latitudo="720" altitudo="720"/>

This file defines no `principale`. A file with a `fenestra` tag and no
`principale` is an app, and briar writes its main function: a pool, an
`AtriumConfiguratio` filled from the tag, `atrium_creare`, one
`internuntius_praebere` for every C region marked `methodus=`, and the
loop that turns the window's crank until it closes. *Atrium* is the
entrance hall of a Roman house, the room every visitor passes through;
here it is the layer that owns the window, the web view and the bridge.
*Internuntius* is a go-between, a messenger sent between two parties —
the word the Church still uses for a papal envoy.

Two facts about that generated main shape everything in the C below.

**Each method is registered with its `datum` set to NIHIL.** The
signature carries a `vacuum* datum` for the program's own state, but
briar v1 always passes nothing there. State that must survive from one
call to the next has to live somewhere else.

**The memory a method answers into is wiped every tick.** Atrium keeps
one arena for calls and resets it at the start of each turn of the
loop. Whatever a method builds there lives exactly long enough to be
written out as JSON and sent to the page, and then it is gone. That is
the right lifetime for an answer. It is the wrong lifetime for a
calendar.

## One exchange across the bridge

Before the code, the conversation it carries. When the window opens,
the page asks for today by sending no date at all; clicking a feast
sends its date. The answer below is real output for 14 September
2026, printed by a copy of this file whose probatio writes it out:

```js
internuntius.vocare("diem_describere", { iso: "2026-09-14" })
```

```json
{
  "iso": "2026-09-14",
  "liturgica": "Feria II, 14 Septembris 2026",
  "romana": "XIV Septembris MMXXVI",
  "pridie": "2026-09-13",
  "postridie": "2026-09-15",
  "hodie": false,
  "titulus": "The Exaltation of the Holy Cross",
  "tempus": "Tempus per Annum",
  "hebdomada": 24,
  "color": "Ruber",
  "color_css": "#9e1b22",
  "celebrationes": [
    {
      "titulus": "Feria II, Hebdomada XXIV",
      "gradus": "Feria",
      "color": "Viridis"
    },
    {
      "titulus": "The Exaltation of the Holy Cross",
      "gradus": "Festum",
      "color": "Ruber"
    }
  ],
  "cyclus": {
    "dominicalis": "A",
    "ferialis": "II",
    "psalterii": 4
  },
  "mysteria": {
    "genus": "Gaudiosa",
    "tituli": [
      "Annuntiatio",
      "Visitatio",
      "Nativitas Domini",
      "Praesentatio in Templo",
      "Inventio in Templo"
    ]
  }
}
```

Two celebrations fall on that Monday: the weekday of the season, green,
and the feast, red. The feast outranks the weekday, so it gives the day
its title and its colour.

The page reads those fields and nothing else. `pridie` and
`postridie` are why the navigation buttons need no arithmetic: C has
already said which days they are.

## The page

### The skeleton

Every element the script will fill has an `id`, and nothing on the
page has content until the first answer arrives. The stylesheet and
the script are linked by name: briar writes `kalendarium.css` and
`kalendarium.js` beside `index.html` in the app's bundle of assets, but
it injects nothing into the page, so the page must ask for them
itself.

The button marked *i* is the imperative of *ire*: go.

<html!>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>kalendarium</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="kalendarium.css">

<header>
    <p id="liturgica" class="supra"></p>
    <h1 id="titulus"></h1>
    <p id="tempus"></p>
    <p id="romana" class="romana"></p>
</header>

<nav>
    <button id="pridie">&larr; pridie</button>
    <button id="hodie">hodie</button>
    <button id="postridie">postridie &rarr;</button>
    <input id="quaesitum" placeholder="AAAA-MM-DD" size="11"
           spellcheck="false">
    <button id="quaerere">i</button>
</nav>
<p id="culpa" class="culpa"></p>

<section>
    <h2>Celebrationes</h2>
    <ul id="celebrationes"></ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Rosarium &middot; <span id="genus-mysteriorum"></span></h2>
    <ol id="mysteria"></ol>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Cycli</h2>
    <p id="cyclus"></p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Festa mobilia anni <span id="annus"></span></h2>
    <ul id="festa" class="festa"></ul>
</section>

<script src="kalendarium.js"></script>
</html>

### The colour of the day

One custom property, `--color-diei`, carries the liturgical colour.
The script sets it from each answer; the band across the top, the
title and the feast links all read it, so the whole page changes
vestments at once. *Charta* is paper, *atramentum* ink, *tenue* the
faint grey of secondary text.

<css!>
:root {
    --color-diei: #2e6b3f;
    --atramentum: #1f1b16;
    --charta: #faf6ee;
    --tenue: #756c60;
}

body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0 32px 32px;
    border-top: 12px solid var(--color-diei);
    background: var(--charta);
    color: var(--atramentum);
    font: 16px/1.45 "Iowan Old Style", Palatino, Georgia, serif;
}

header {
    padding-top: 20px;
}

.supra, .romana, #tempus {
    margin: 0;
}

.supra, .romana {
    color: var(--tenue);
}

.romana {
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
}

h1 {
    margin: 4px 0 2px;
    font-size: 28px;
    font-weight: normal;
    color: var(--color-diei);
}

h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 6px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: normal;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--tenue);
}

nav {
    display: flex;
    gap: 6px;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
}

button, input {
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 14px;
}

.culpa {
    min-height: 1.3em;
    margin: 2px 0;
    color: #9e1b22;
}

ul, ol {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.4em;
}

#mysteria {
    list-style-type: upper-roman;
}

.glossa {
    color: var(--tenue);
    font-size: 14px;
}

.festa button {
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    background: none;
    color: var(--color-diei);
    font-size: 16px;
    text-decoration: underline;
    cursor: pointer;
}
</css>

### Asking, and drawing the answer

`internuntius.vocare(methodus, argumenta)` is the whole bridge from
this side. It returns a promise. If the C method gives back a value,
the promise resolves with it; if the method sets `culpa`, the promise
rejects with an `Error` whose message is that text. So a malformed
date typed into the field needs no checking here: C refuses it, and
`culpam_ostendere` prints the refusal under the buttons.

The script keeps one thing, `ultimum`, the last answer. The arrow
buttons and the arrow keys read `pridie` and `postridie` from it. The
year's feasts are asked for only when the year on screen changes.

`romanus` writes the week and the year in Roman numerals, because the
page should not show `23` beside `Hebdomada XXIII`.

<js!>
(function () {
    "use strict";

    var ultimum = null;
    var annus_monstratus = null;

    function el(id) {
        return document.getElementById(id);
    }

    function scribere(id, textus) {
        el(id).textContent = textus;
    }

    function vacuare(id) {
        var nodus = el(id);
        while (nodus.firstChild) {
            nodus.removeChild(nodus.firstChild);
        }
        return nodus;
    }

    function romanus(n) {
        var valores = [1000, 900, 500, 400, 100, 90, 50, 40, 10, 9, 5, 4, 1];
        var signa = ["M", "CM", "D", "CD", "C", "XC", "L", "XL", "X", "IX",
                     "V", "IV", "I"];
        var textus = "";
        var i;
        for (i = 0; i < valores.length; i++) {
            while (n >= valores[i]) {
                textus += signa[i];
                n -= valores[i];
            }
        }
        return textus;
    }

    function addere(lista, textus, glossa) {
        var li = document.createElement("li");
        var parva;
        li.textContent = textus;
        if (glossa) {
            parva = document.createElement("span");
            parva.className = "glossa";
            parva.textContent = " — " + glossa;
            li.appendChild(parva);
        }
        lista.appendChild(li);
    }

    function culpam_ostendere(e) {
        scribere("culpa", e.message);
    }

    function diem_ostendere(r) {
        var lista;

        ultimum = r;
        document.documentElement.style.setProperty("--color-diei",
                                                   r.color_css);
        scribere("liturgica", r.liturgica);
        scribere("titulus", r.titulus);
        scribere("tempus", r.hebdomada > 0
            ? r.tempus + ", hebdomada " + romanus(r.hebdomada)
            : r.tempus);
        scribere("romana", r.romana);
        scribere("culpa", "");
        el("hodie").disabled = r.hodie;

        lista = vacuare("celebrationes");
        r.celebrationes.forEach(function (c) {
            addere(lista, c.titulus, c.gradus + ", " + c.color.toLowerCase());
        });

        scribere("genus-mysteriorum", r.mysteria.genus);
        lista = vacuare("mysteria");
        r.mysteria.tituli.forEach(function (t) {
            addere(lista, t);
        });

        scribere("cyclus", "Cyclus dominicalis " + r.cyclus.dominicalis
            + " · ferialis " + r.cyclus.ferialis
            + " · hebdomada psalterii " + romanus(r.cyclus.psalterii));

        if (r.iso.slice(0, 4) !== annus_monstratus) {
            annum_petere(r.iso);
        }
    }

    function annum_ostendere(r) {
        var lista = vacuare("festa");

        annus_monstratus = String(r.annus);
        scribere("annus", romanus(r.annus));
        r.festa.forEach(function (f) {
            var li = document.createElement("li");
            var bulla = document.createElement("button");
            bulla.textContent = f.titulus;
            bulla.onclick = function () { petere(f.iso); };
            li.appendChild(bulla);
            li.appendChild(document.createTextNode(" " + f.brevis));
            lista.appendChild(li);
        });
    }

    function petere(iso) {
        internuntius.vocare("diem_describere", iso ? { iso: iso } : {})
            .then(diem_ostendere)
            .catch(culpam_ostendere);
    }

    function annum_petere(iso) {
        internuntius.vocare("annum_describere", { iso: iso })
            .then(annum_ostendere)
            .catch(culpam_ostendere);
    }

    function gradi(clavis) {
        if (ultimum) {
            petere(ultimum[clavis]);
        }
    }

    el("pridie").onclick = function () { gradi("pridie"); };
    el("postridie").onclick = function () { gradi("postridie"); };
    el("hodie").onclick = function () { petere(null); };
    el("quaerere").onclick = function () {
        petere(el("quaesitum").value.trim());
    };
    el("quaesitum").addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if (e.key === "Enter") {
            el("quaerere").click();
        }
    });
    document.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if (e.target === el("quaesitum")) {
            return;
        }
        if (e.key === "ArrowLeft") {
            gradi("pridie");
        } else if (e.key === "ArrowRight") {
            gradi("postridie");
        }
    });

    petere(null);
}());
</js>

## The C

### The one include this file writes

briar derives every header this file needs except one. The methods
speak JSON, and `json.h` is not found: the first build of this file
stopped at `unknown type name 'JsonValor'`. The cause is the check on
method signatures. To compare each `methodus=` function against the
bridge's type, briar parses that region with `internuntius.h` already
included, so `JsonValor` never looks unknown to it and nothing adds
its header to the generated one. `salve_vitreum.thistle` carries the
same line for the same reason. Written once, it reaches
every region, because briar gathers the directives of all C regions
into one generated header.

<c!>
#include "json.h"
</c>

### A calendar that outlives the call

`calendarium_creare` takes a pool and keeps it. The calendar computes
each year's movable feasts once — Easter by the computus, and every
feast that hangs from it — and caches them in that pool, so the second
question about a year costs nothing. Handed the call arena, the cache
would be wiped at the next tick while the calendar still pointed into
it.

So the calendar gets a pool of its own, made on the first call and
never emptied. Both live at file scope, *hic manens*, because the
`datum` slot that would normally carry them is NIHIL. briar places
file-scope objects in the generated `kalendarium_regiones.c`, where
the methods are, and nowhere else can see them; everything outside
reaches the calendar through `kalendarium_obtinere`.

<c!>
hic_manens Piscina*               piscina_perpetua      = NIHIL;
hic_manens CalendariumLiturgicum* kalendarium_perpetuum = NIHIL;

CalendariumLiturgicum*
kalendarium_obtinere (vacuum)
{
    si (kalendarium_perpetuum == NIHIL)
    {
        piscina_perpetua = piscina_generare_dynamicum("kalendarium",
                                                      LXIV * M);
        si (piscina_perpetua == NIHIL)
        {
            redde NIHIL;
        }
        kalendarium_perpetuum = calendarium_creare(piscina_perpetua);
    }
    redde kalendarium_perpetuum;
}
</c>

### Two fragments both methods share

Both methods begin the same way: find out which day was asked about,
then make sure the calendar exists. Each of those is written once, as
a fragment, and woven into both methods — one text in two places, not
two copies that can drift apart.

The day comes from the `iso` field. If the page sent none, the day is
today. If it sent something that is not a string, or a string that is
not a real date, the method sets `culpa` and gives back nothing, and
the bridge turns that into a rejected promise on the page.
`fasti_parsare_iso` does the work in one call: it reads `AAAA-MM-DD`
and then checks the date exists, so `2026-02-30` is refused along with
`quinta`.

<c! id="dies-ex-argumentis">
iso = json_objectum_capere(argumenta, "iso");
si (iso == NIHIL)
{
    dies = fasti_dies_hodie();
}
alioquin si (!json_est_chorda(iso)
         || !fasti_parsare_iso(json_ad_chorda(iso), &dies))
{
    *culpa = chorda_ex_literis(
        "dies non intellecta: forma AAAA-MM-DD exspectatur", piscina);
    redde NIHIL;
}
</c>

`json_objectum_capere` is safe to call on `argumenta` even when the
page sent nothing and `argumenta` is NIHIL: it returns NIHIL, and the
day becomes today.

<c! id="kalendarium-paratum">
cal = kalendarium_obtinere();
si (cal == NIHIL)
{
    *culpa = chorda_ex_literis("kalendarium creari non potuit", piscina);
    redde NIHIL;
}
</c>

### Diem describere: the day

*Describere* is to write out, to copy down; *diem describere*, to
write out the day. This is the method the page calls on every step.

Read the cast list as the plan: a calendar, the information it gives
about one day, the day itself, the argument it came from, and the
answer being built. The body finds the day, readies the calendar, asks
the calendar, and then fills the answer in five parts.

<c! methodus="diem_describere">
JsonValor*
diem_describere (
    JsonValor* argumenta,
      Piscina* piscina,
       vacuum* datum,
       chorda* culpa)
{
    CalendariumLiturgicum* cal;
           InformatioDiei* info;
                      Dies  dies;
                JsonValor* iso;
                JsonValor* fructus;

    (vacuum)datum;
    <<#dies-ex-argumentis>>
    <<#kalendarium-paratum>>
    info = calendarium_obtinere_diem(cal, dies, piscina);
    si (info == NIHIL)
    {
        *culpa = chorda_ex_literis("dies describi non potuit", piscina);
        redde NIHIL;
    }

    fructus = json_objectum_creare(piscina);
    <<#fructus-diei>>
    <<#fructus-temporis>>
    json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "celebrationes",
        celebrationes_describere(info, piscina));
    json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "cyclus",
        cyclum_describere(info, piscina));
    json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "mysteria",
        mysteria_describere(fasti_dies_hebdomadis(dies), piscina));
    redde fructus;
}
</c>

Note where the answer is built: in `piscina`, the call arena. That is
also where `calendarium_obtinere_diem` puts the day's information, and
the only thing the answer points into besides constant tables. The
whole answer is written out as JSON before the next tick wipes the
arena, which is exactly the life it needs.

#### The date, four ways

The same day, written as the page needs it: `iso` for asking again,
the liturgical form for the top line, the Roman form with its numerals
for the line under the title, and the neighbouring days. `hodie` tells
the page whether to grey out its *hodie* button.

<c! id="fructus-diei">
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "iso",
    valor_diei(dies, FASTI_FORMA_ISO, piscina));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "liturgica",
    valor_diei(dies, FASTI_FORMA_LITURGICA, piscina));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "romana",
    valor_diei(dies, FASTI_FORMA_LATINA_LONGA, piscina));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "pridie",
    valor_diei(fasti_addere_dies(dies, -I), FASTI_FORMA_ISO, piscina));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "postridie",
    valor_diei(fasti_addere_dies(dies, I), FASTI_FORMA_ISO, piscina));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "hodie", json_boolean_creare(piscina,
    fasti_aequalis(dies, fasti_dies_hodie())));
</c>

`valor_diei` is the one line those five share: format the day, wrap
the text as a JSON string.

<c!>
JsonValor*
valor_diei (
        Dies  dies,
         s32  forma,
    Piscina* piscina)
{
    redde json_chorda_creare(piscina,
                             fasti_formare_diem(dies, forma, piscina));
}
</c>

`json_chorda_creare` does not copy the text; it keeps the `chorda` it
is given, pointer and length. That is safe here because
`fasti_formare_diem` allocates the text in the same call arena. Handed
a buffer on the stack, the same call would send the page whatever the
stack held by the time the answer was written.

#### The season and the colour

`titulus_diei` is the calendar's name for the day: *Sabbatum,
Hebdomada XXIII* on an ordinary Saturday, or the saint's name when a
feast outranks the weekday. The colour goes over twice, once as the
calendar names it and once as the page should paint it.

<c! id="fructus-temporis">
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "titulus",
    json_chorda_creare(piscina, info->titulus_diei));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "tempus", json_chorda_creare(piscina,
    calendarium_nomen_temporis(info->tempus_info.tempus, FALSUM, piscina)));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "hebdomada", json_integer_creare(piscina,
    (s64)info->tempus_info.hebdomada));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "color", json_chorda_creare(piscina,
    calendarium_nomen_coloris(info->color_diei, piscina)));
json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "color_css",
    json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina, color_paginae(info->color_diei)));
</c>

The page colours come from a table indexed by the calendar's own
`ColorLiturgicus`, so the rows follow the order of that enumeration in
`calendarium_liturgicum.h`: white, red, green, violet, rose, black.

White is drawn as gold. A white band on a white page cannot be seen,
and gold is the colour that stands in for white on solemn days.

<c!>
hic_manens constans character* constans COLORES_PAGINAE[VI] = {
    "#9a7b2f",    /* albus, pictus ut aurum */
    "#9e1b22",    /* ruber */
    "#2e6b3f",    /* viridis */
    "#5a2a6e",    /* violaceus */
    "#b76e8c",    /* rosaceus */
    "#2b2b2b"     /* niger */
};

constans character*
color_paginae (
    ColorLiturgicus color)
{
    si ((s32)color < ZEPHYRUM || (s32)color > (s32)COLLIT_NIGER)
    {
        redde COLORES_PAGINAE[II];
    }
    redde COLORES_PAGINAE[color];
}
</c>

#### The celebrations

A day can carry more than one celebration: the weekday of the season,
and any saints whose rank lets them be kept on it. The calendar has
already done the ranking; this only lists what it kept, each with its
rank and colour by name.

<c!>
JsonValor*
celebrationes_describere (
    constans InformatioDiei* info,
                   Piscina* piscina)
{
    JsonValor* tabula;
    JsonValor* celebratio;
          s32  i;

    tabula = json_tabulatum_creare(piscina);
    per (i = ZEPHYRUM; i < info->numerus_celebrationum; i++)
    {
        constans Celebratio* c = &info->celebrationes[i];

        celebratio = json_objectum_creare(piscina);
        json_objectum_ponere(celebratio, "titulus",
            json_chorda_creare(piscina, c->titulus));
        json_objectum_ponere(celebratio, "gradus",
            json_chorda_creare(piscina,
                calendarium_nomen_gradus(c->gradus, FALSUM, piscina)));
        json_objectum_ponere(celebratio, "color",
            json_chorda_creare(piscina,
                calendarium_nomen_coloris(c->color, piscina)));
        json_tabulatum_addere(tabula, celebratio);
    }
    redde tabula;
}
</c>

#### The cycles

<c!>
hic_manens constans character* constans CYCLI_DOMINICALES[III] = {
    "A", "B", "C"
};

hic_manens constans character* constans CYCLI_FERIALES[II] = {
    "I", "II"
};

JsonValor*
cyclum_describere (
    constans InformatioDiei* info,
                   Piscina* piscina)
{
    JsonValor* cyclus = json_objectum_creare(piscina);

    json_objectum_ponere(cyclus, "dominicalis",
        json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina,
            CYCLI_DOMINICALES[info->cyclus.cyclus_dominicalis]));
    json_objectum_ponere(cyclus, "ferialis",
        json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina,
            CYCLI_FERIALES[info->cyclus.cyclus_quotidianus]));
    json_objectum_ponere(cyclus, "psalterii", json_integer_creare(piscina,
        (s64)info->cyclus.hebdomada_psalterii));
    redde cyclus;
}
</c>

#### The mysteries of the Rosary

The mysteries are four sets of five: *Gaudiosa*, the joyful;
*Luminosa*, the luminous; *Dolorosa*, the sorrowful; *Gloriosa*, the
glorious. John Paul II added the luminous mysteries in *Rosarium
Virginis Mariae* (2002), and the same letter gives the days: joyful on
Monday and Saturday, sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday, glorious on
Wednesday and Sunday, luminous on Thursday.

That rule is a table of seven, written with names so it can be checked
against the sentence above by eye. It begins on Sunday, because
`fasti` counts the week from the Lord's day: `FASTI_DOMINICA` is zero.

<c!>
nomen enumeratio {
    MYSTERIA_GAUDIOSA = ZEPHYRUM,
    MYSTERIA_LUMINOSA = I,
    MYSTERIA_DOLOROSA = II,
    MYSTERIA_GLORIOSA = III
} GenusMysteriorum;

hic_manens constans GenusMysteriorum GENUS_PER_DIEM[VII] = {
    MYSTERIA_GLORIOSA,    /* Dominica */
    MYSTERIA_GAUDIOSA,    /* Feria II */
    MYSTERIA_DOLOROSA,    /* Feria III */
    MYSTERIA_GLORIOSA,    /* Feria IV */
    MYSTERIA_LUMINOSA,    /* Feria V */
    MYSTERIA_DOLOROSA,    /* Feria VI */
    MYSTERIA_GAUDIOSA     /* Sabbatum */
};

hic_manens constans character* constans GENERA_MYSTERIORUM[IV] = {
    "Gaudiosa", "Luminosa", "Dolorosa", "Gloriosa"
};

hic_manens constans character* constans TITULI_MYSTERIORUM[IV][V] = {
    { "Annuntiatio", "Visitatio", "Nativitas Domini",
      "Praesentatio in Templo", "Inventio in Templo" },
    { "Baptisma in Iordane", "Nuptiae in Cana", "Proclamatio Regni",
      "Transfiguratio", "Institutio Eucharistiae" },
    { "Agonia in Horto", "Flagellatio", "Coronatio Spinis",
      "Baiulatio Crucis", "Crucifixio" },
    { "Resurrectio", "Ascensio", "Descensus Spiritus Sancti",
      "Assumptio", "Coronatio in Caelo" }
};

GenusMysteriorum
genus_mysteriorum (
    s32 dies_hebdomadis)
{
    si (dies_hebdomadis < ZEPHYRUM || dies_hebdomadis > VI)
    {
        redde MYSTERIA_GAUDIOSA;
    }
    redde GENUS_PER_DIEM[dies_hebdomadis];
}

JsonValor*
mysteria_describere (
         s32  dies_hebdomadis,
    Piscina* piscina)
{
          JsonValor* mysteria;
          JsonValor* tituli;
    GenusMysteriorum  genus;
                 s32  i;

    genus    = genus_mysteriorum(dies_hebdomadis);
    mysteria = json_objectum_creare(piscina);
    tituli   = json_tabulatum_creare(piscina);
    json_objectum_ponere(mysteria, "genus",
        json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina, GENERA_MYSTERIORUM[genus]));
    per (i = ZEPHYRUM; i < V; i++)
    {
        json_tabulatum_addere(tituli, json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina,
            TITULI_MYSTERIORUM[genus][i]));
    }
    json_objectum_ponere(mysteria, "tituli", tituli);
    redde mysteria;
}
</c>

### Annum describere: the feasts that move

Easter falls on the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after
the spring equinox, as the Church reckons both, and a whole season of
feasts is counted from it. `calendarium_anchorae` computes those
anchors for a year once and caches them in the calendar's own pool —
the reason, above, that the calendar has one.

The second method takes the same argument as the first, a day, and
answers for that day's year. The page asks for it only when the year
on screen changes.

<c! methodus="annum_describere">
JsonValor*
annum_describere (
    JsonValor* argumenta,
      Piscina* piscina,
       vacuum* datum,
       chorda* culpa)
{
    CalendariumLiturgicum* cal;
          AnchoresMobiles* a;
                      Dies  dies;
                JsonValor* iso;
                JsonValor* festa;
                JsonValor* fructus;

    (vacuum)datum;
    <<#dies-ex-argumentis>>
    <<#kalendarium-paratum>>
    a = calendarium_anchorae(cal, dies.annus);
    si (a == NIHIL)
    {
        *culpa = chorda_ex_literis("festa mobilia computari non potuerunt",
                                   piscina);
        redde NIHIL;
    }

    festa = json_tabulatum_creare(piscina);
    <<#festa-anni>>
    fructus = json_objectum_creare(piscina);
    json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "annus",
        json_integer_creare(piscina, (s64)dies.annus));
    json_objectum_ponere(fructus, "festa", festa);
    redde fructus;
}
</c>

Eleven feasts, in the order they fall in the year. The names are the
Latin ones, written here; the dates are the calendar's.

<c! id="festa-anni">
festum_addere(festa, "Baptisma Domini",         a->baptisma_domini,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Feria IV Cinerum",        a->feria_iv_cinerum,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Dominica in Palmis",      a->dominica_palmarum,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Dominica Resurrectionis", a->pascha,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Ascensio Domini",         a->ascensio,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Pentecoste",              a->pentecoste,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Sanctissima Trinitas",    a->ss_trinitas,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Corpus Christi",          a->corpus_christi,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Sacratissimum Cor Iesu",  a->cor_iesu,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Christus Rex",            a->christus_rex,
              piscina);
festum_addere(festa, "Dominica I Adventus",     a->dominica_i_adventus,
              piscina);
</c>

<c!>
vacuum
festum_addere (
             JsonValor* festa,
    constans character* titulus,
                   Dies  dies,
               Piscina* piscina)
{
    JsonValor* festum = json_objectum_creare(piscina);

    json_objectum_ponere(festum, "titulus",
        json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina, titulus));
    json_objectum_ponere(festum, "iso",
        valor_diei(dies, FASTI_FORMA_ISO, piscina));
    json_objectum_ponere(festum, "brevis",
        valor_diei(dies, FASTI_FORMA_LATINA_BREVIS, piscina));
    json_tabulatum_addere(festa, festum);
}
</c>

## Probatio

The probatio opens no window. It calls the two methods directly, the
way the bridge would, with arguments built by hand, and reads the
answers the way the page would. That covers everything except the
page itself.

Two small helpers keep the checks readable. `campus` reads a string
field; for a missing field it gives back an empty string rather than
failing, because `json_ad_chorda` does, so a missing field fails its
assertion instead of crashing the test.

<c! munus="probatio">
interior JsonValor*
argumenta_diei (
    constans character* iso,
               Piscina* piscina)
{
    JsonValor* argumenta = json_objectum_creare(piscina);

    json_objectum_ponere(argumenta, "iso",
        json_chorda_creare_literis(piscina, iso));
    redde argumenta;
}

interior chorda
campus (
             JsonValor* objectum,
    constans character* clavis)
{
    redde json_ad_chorda(json_objectum_capere(objectum, clavis));
}

s32
principale (vacuum)
{
      Piscina* piscina;
    JsonValor* r;
    JsonValor* festa;
    JsonValor* argumenta;
        chorda  culpa;

    piscina = piscina_generare_dynamicum("probatio", LXIV * M);
    credo_aperire(piscina);
    culpa.datum   = NIHIL;
    culpa.mensura = ZEPHYRUM;

    <<#probatio-pascha>>
    <<#probatio-festum>>
    <<#probatio-mysteria>>
    <<#probatio-culpa>>
    <<#probatio-hodie>>
    <<#probatio-annus>>

    credo_imprimere_compendium();
    redde credo_omnia_praeterierunt() ? ZEPHYRUM : I;
}
</c>

**Easter 2026.** The fifth of April is a Sunday. It must be Paschal
time, the colour white, the neighbouring days the fourth and the
sixth, and the mysteries glorious.

<c! id="probatio-pascha">
r = diem_describere(argumenta_diei("2026-04-05", piscina), piscina,
                    NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NON_NIHIL(r);
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "tempus"), "Tempus Paschale");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "color"), "Albus");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "pridie"), "2026-04-04");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "postridie"), "2026-04-06");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_objectum_capere(r, "mysteria"), "genus"), "Gloriosa");
</c>

**A feast that outranks the weekday.** The Exaltation of the Holy
Cross falls on 14 September, a Monday in 2026. It is a feast, which
outranks a weekday in Ordinary Time, so the day turns red, and the
page colour with it — while the mysteries stay those of a Monday.
It is also Monday of the twenty-fourth week, psalter week IV, and its
dates read *Feria II, 14 Septembris 2026* and *XIV Septembris MMXXVI*:
four answers this file's first build got wrong, before the calendar
and `fasti` were fixed (see the end of the file).

<c! id="probatio-festum">
r = diem_describere(argumenta_diei("2026-09-14", piscina), piscina,
                    NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NON_NIHIL(r);
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "color"), "Ruber");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "color_css"), "#9e1b22");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "liturgica"),
                              "Feria II, 14 Septembris 2026");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(campus(r, "romana"),
                              "XIV Septembris MMXXVI");
CREDO_AEQUALIS_S64(json_ad_integer(json_objectum_capere(r, "hebdomada")),
                   (s64)XXIV);
CREDO_AEQUALIS_S64(json_ad_integer(json_objectum_capere(
    json_objectum_capere(r, "cyclus"), "psalterii")), (s64)IV);
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_objectum_capere(r, "mysteria"), "genus"), "Gaudiosa");
</c>

**The weekday rule, at its two lone days.** Thursday is the only
luminous day, and Wednesday shares glory with Sunday.

<c! id="probatio-mysteria">
CREDO_AEQUALIS_S32((s32)genus_mysteriorum(FASTI_FERIA_V),
                   (s32)MYSTERIA_LUMINOSA);
CREDO_AEQUALIS_S32((s32)genus_mysteriorum(FASTI_FERIA_IV),
                   (s32)MYSTERIA_GLORIOSA);
</c>

**A day that does not exist, and a date that is not text.** Both must
set `culpa` and give back nothing — the two ways the first fragment
refuses.

<c! id="probatio-culpa">
r = diem_describere(argumenta_diei("2026-02-30", piscina), piscina,
                    NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NIHIL(r);
CREDO_CHORDA_NON_VACUA(culpa);

culpa.mensura = ZEPHYRUM;
argumenta = json_objectum_creare(piscina);
json_objectum_ponere(argumenta, "iso",
    json_integer_creare(piscina, (s64)20260405));
r = diem_describere(argumenta, piscina, NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NIHIL(r);
CREDO_CHORDA_NON_VACUA(culpa);
</c>

**No argument at all means today.** This is the call the page makes
when the window opens.

<c! id="probatio-hodie">
r = diem_describere(NIHIL, piscina, NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NON_NIHIL(r);
CREDO_VERUM(json_ad_boolean(json_objectum_capere(r, "hodie")));
</c>

**The year's feasts.** Eleven, in order, and four of them checked
against dates known without this calendar: Ash Wednesday is Easter
less forty-six days, Pentecost Easter plus forty-nine, and the first
Sunday of Advent the fourth Sunday before Christmas, which in 2026
falls on a Friday.

<c! id="probatio-annus">
r = annum_describere(argumenta_diei("2026-09-12", piscina), piscina,
                     NIHIL, &culpa);
CREDO_NON_NIHIL(r);
festa = json_objectum_capere(r, "festa");
CREDO_AEQUALIS_I32(json_tabulatum_numerus(festa), XI);
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_tabulatum_obtinere(festa, I), "iso"), "2026-02-18");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_tabulatum_obtinere(festa, III), "iso"), "2026-04-05");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_tabulatum_obtinere(festa, V), "iso"), "2026-05-24");
CREDO_CHORDA_AEQUALIS_LITERIS(
    campus(json_tabulatum_obtinere(festa, X), "iso"), "2026-11-29");
</c>

## Consilia: what was considered and not done

**What building this file found.** An example that puts a library on
screen is a test that the library's own suite was not. The first builds
of this file turned up four defects, each fixed in its own commit with
its own gate:

- the build closure never collected `lib/sanctorale.c`, which
  `calendarium_liturgicum.h` names in an annotation, so the app did not
  link (`41e1d0c8`, in `lib/silex.c`);
- every day of Ordinary Time after Pentecost was "week I", psalter week
  I (`0cb8d33d`);
- the reading cycles lagged a year from Advent to 31 December
  (`caf2fc0f`);
- the Latin date forms wrote the month in the nominative, and the long
  form had no Roman numerals (`11d6d6c4`).

One more stays open and is worked around above: briar does not derive
`json.h` for method regions. And the calendar still gives a movable
solemnity the season's title — Christ the King reads *Dominica XXXIV
per Annum*.

**The page does no date arithmetic.** It could: JavaScript has a
`Date`, and "the day after" looks like one line. But `Date` works in
milliseconds and the local time zone, and a day stepped across a
change of clocks can land an hour short of midnight and print the
same date twice. C already has a calendar that counts in days. So C
names the neighbouring days in every answer, and the page only ever
repeats a date it was given.

**State lives at file scope because `datum` is NIHIL.** In a
hand-written app the calendar would ride in the `datum` pointer given
to `internuntius_praebere`, and the methods would find it there. briar
v1 always registers methods with NIHIL, and the richer arrangement is a
named deferral in its spec. For one window in one process, a
file-scope object is honest: there is exactly one calendar, and it is
where the methods can reach it.

**The saints' names are English.** The seasons, the weekdays and the
ranks come out of the calendar in Latin; the saints come from
`lib/sanctorale.c`, whose table is in English. Translating them in this
file would fork the calendar's data into a second copy that must be
kept in step by hand. The right place to fix it is the table.

**White is painted gold.** A literal white would vanish on the page.

**Sundays of Advent and Lent.** A long custom prays the joyful
mysteries on the Sundays of Advent and the sorrowful on the Sundays of
Lent. This program keeps the plain weekday rule of *Rosarium Virginis
Mariae*, which itself leaves room for such customs.

**The page is untested.** The probatio proves every answer the page
can receive, but not that the page draws it. A test that clicks the
buttons exists as a pattern — `tools/briar_fumus.sh -agere` drives a
window through `bin/manus` — and would be the next step if this file
became more than an example.

**No single-file export.** `briar -amalgama` writes a thistle out as one
C file that clang compiles alone. It refuses vitrea programs in v1:
the web view needs Objective-C and a bundle of assets that one `.c`
file cannot carry.
